dialect grammar
grammar stdio.dxf.snapshot
extension dxf
start document
comment none

# P2-FG1: real DXF R12 ASCII group-code/value line-pair grammar, replacing the old ABNF-dialect
# placeholder (wrong header syntax entirely — `dialect grammar stdio.dxf.snapshot` on one line,
# `;` comments, `/` alternation, `%x` char ranges, none of which this repo's real `parse_grammar`
# dialect understands). Matches this artifact's own real tokenizer 1:1
# (🏅️standards/🔖️r12/🪆️subsets/📰️header/🧬️schema/📸️snapshot/🦀️.rs `tokenize_dxf`:322-338):
# every DXF tag is exactly TWO physical lines — an integer group-code line, then an arbitrary-text
# value line. `LINE` (P2-M1's raw-span terminal) is the exact primitive `tokenize_dxf`'s own doc
# comment at `📖️grammar/🦀️.rs`'s `RawSpanEnd::Newline` cites BY NAME ("dxf's opaque
# group-code value lines") — it captures the value line's rest-of-physical-line VERBATIM from the
# original source text, past whatever the shared lexer already fragmented it into (arbitrary
# punctuation like `SOME TEXT, with punctuation!`, or a `$`-prefixed header var name like
# `$ACADVER` — `$` never needs its own promoted-token handling here because `LINE` never
# re-tokenizes its span at all, sidestepping the W0 recon's item (2) concern by construction, not
# by declaring the `DOLLAR` terminal). `comment none` because a real value line may legally contain
# a literal `#` (e.g. a `TEXT` entity's string value) — same reasoning csv/txt's own grammars use.
#
# Per-code semantic typing (string vs int vs double vs point, chosen by an EARLIER group code —
# W0 recon item (3)) stays exactly what the mission brief calls it: a Rust-side POST-PARSE concern
# (`classify_group_code_value`), not a lexical-shape decision this grammar needs to encode — this
# grammar's only job is recognizing the real (code-line, value-line) pair shape, genuinely, which
# it does for every section (HEADER/TABLES/BLOCKS/ENTITIES) and every nested record (table entries,
# block headers, entity bodies, POLYLINE/VERTEX/SEQEND) uniformly, since they all share the same
# flat two-line tag shape at the tokenizer level — section/record structure is a Rust-side walk
# over the flat tag stream (`parse_header_section`/`parse_tables_section`/`parse_blocks_section`/
# `parse_entities_until`), not a grammar-level nesting concern (same "flat token stream, walked by
# Rust" split obj/stl/txt's own grammars use for their own record-oriented formats).
#
# `document` matches the m5 harness's own reconstructed body shape: the mandatory
# `semio stdio.dxf.dsl v1` preamble line is stripped and replaced with the bare `envelope_id()`
# token before recognition (`grammar_conformance_law`, `⚙️engine/🦀️.rs`), so
# `artifact-mark` below matches literally `stdio.dxf` (one token — `.` is an ident-continue
# character in this lexer), not the full preamble line — same convention json's own snapshot
# grammar uses.
artifact-mark = "stdio.dxf"
document = artifact-mark tag*
tag = INT LINE
